# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: cador-bricscad-windows-log-encoding — clautolisp's LOGFILEMODE log is UTF-8 under the BricsCAD dialects; BricsCAD V25 on Windows writes windows-1252
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: divergence
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: fix-cador-bricscad-windows-log-encoding
#+DATE: 2026-10-08

* Context

Filed 2026-10-08 from the encoding measurements recorded in
=encoding-situations-cli-options= (section "Measured 2026-10-08"). clautolisp
writes per-drawing command-history logs (clautolisp 2.2.209,
=clautolisp/cador/source/command-log.lisp=), and =%command-log-encoding=
picks the encoding: =-Elog= when given, else windows-1252 for the AutoCAD
product and UTF-8 otherwise -- so UTF-8 under every BricsCAD dialect. At the
time BricsCAD V25's log encoding was not settled (windows-1252 or UTF-8 with a
BOM).

* Problem

Measured now (job 16998925784, encoding:experiment:bricscad:windows, pipeline
2922145991, BricsCAD V25 (x64) fr, Windows): the raw log
=Dessin1_2026-10-08_11-54-58.log= is 475 bytes; its first 16 bytes are
=0D 0A 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 2D 20 5B 20 42= (no BOM), and its first byte
above 7F, at offset 143, sits in =0D 0A 44 E9 70 61 72 74= ("Depart" with
e-acute as the single byte E9). BricsCAD V25 on Windows therefore writes
windows-1252 without a BOM. clautolisp under a BricsCAD dialect writes UTF-8,
so a program comparing logs, or a tool reading clautolisp's log the way it
reads BricsCAD's, sees different bytes on Windows.

BricsCAD V26 on macOS writes no log in batch mode (job 16998925783), so the
macOS BricsCAD dialects have no measured encoding (UTF-8 is the platform's
natural choice and stays).

Repro: =clautolisp-sbcl --dialect bricscad-v25= on Windows, =(setvar
"LOGFILEMODE" 1)=, run a command whose prompt carries a non-ASCII character,
look at the bytes of =(getvar "LOGFILENAME")=.

* Proposal

In =%command-log-encoding=: windows-1252 for the BricsCAD product when the
dialect's platform is Windows (dispatch on the product and platform, never on
a dialect keyword -- AGENTS.md, "Dialect versions and products"); UTF-8 for
the macOS / Linux BricsCAD dialects. Update the clautolisp user manual's log
table (the "Known gap" note added 2026-10-08) and the LOGFILEMODE
=*** clautolisp= note. Not done on the measuring branch because the cador
sources were being edited on parallel branches.

* Acceptance criteria

- under a Windows BricsCAD dialect, with no -Elog, an e-acute in the log is
  the byte E9, and no BOM is written;
- the macOS BricsCAD and the other dialects are unchanged;
- unit test in the cador suite; manual note removed.
